$DIR/main.p:11:2: union Slot must be declared owner: its variant Full holds an owned[int], which is an owner
    An owner owns memory or another resource and releases it when it is dropped,
    so it moves instead of being copied. A union holding one owns the payload of
    the variant it holds: it drops it when the union is dropped or given another
    variant, and the union moves instead of being copied.
    Go copies such values freely and its garbage collector frees what they refer
    to when nothing refers to it any more. Plutonium has no garbage collector:
    an owner frees what it owns when it is dropped, so there must be exactly
    one.
    Plutonium does not make Slot an owner silently; say so in its declaration:
        type Slot owner union {
            ...
        }
    If the union should only look at a value something else owns, hold a
    pointer:
        Full *int
    (see "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13)
    (run "pluto explain E2406" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:22:2: missing case in type switch on st (DeviceState): Failed (add it or a default case)
    A type switch on a union value that has no default case must name every
    variant. That way, adding a variant to the union shows every switch that
    needs to handle it.
    Add a case for each missing variant, or a default case if the remaining
    variants share one action. A switch that names every variant needs no
    default to end a function.
    Go cannot check this for interfaces, whose set of types is open. A union's
    variants are a closed set, so the compiler can tell when one is forgotten.
    (see "Switch statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2414" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:32:2: type switch on packed union d (Desc) needs a default case: its 2-bit tag kind can hold 4 values, and its cases cover 2
    A packed union's bits usually come from hardware or from converting a raw
    value, so its tag may hold any value its bits allow, including values no
    variant names. A type switch without a default case must therefore cover
    every value of the tag; this one does not.
    Add a default case that handles the other values.
    (see "Switch statements" in the Plutonium spec, section 12.9)
    (run "pluto explain E2415" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:42:20: DeviceState.Probing is a variant of union DeviceState, not a type (its payload has type struct{attempt int})
    A variant of a union is not a type. U.V(x) and U.V{...} make a value of the
    union type U holding the variant, and a type switch on a union gives you the
    payload of the variant it holds, which has the payload's own type.
    To keep a payload in a variable, give the variable the payload's type.
    Go has no unions. Keeping variants out of the type system means a payload
    always has its own type and methods, and generic unions need no special
    rules.
    (see "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13)
    (run "pluto explain E2409" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:43:8: cannot write a composite literal of union type DeviceState: name the variant it holds (Off, Probing and Failed)
    A union value holds one of its variants, so a literal must name the variant:
    U.V{...} for a variant whose payload is a struct, array or packed type, or
    U.V(payload) for any variant with a payload. A variant without a payload is
    the value U.V itself. In a packed union, the variant sets the tag, so its
    literal cannot.
    (see "Unions" in the Plutonium spec, section 7.13)
    (run "pluto explain E2412" for a longer explanation)
